package ui

// The dialog primitive (UI §7.7, UI §8.1 tier 4, UI §11's risk table).
//
// A dialog is the one component in this package that is modal, which is why its
// markup carries more state than anything else here: the panel is `hidden` until
// something opens it, it is `aria-modal`, it is named by its own heading, and it
// is the target of its trigger's `aria-controls`. Every one of those is a
// *server-rendered* half of a contract whose other half is a script that does not
// exist until phase 9 — so each attribute here is written now, against the
// contract it has to satisfy, and asserted against the markup rather than
// against behaviour.

// DialogView is a modal layer: the trigger that opens it and the panel it opens.
//
// The two travel together because §7.7's contract is *between* them — the
// trigger's `aria-controls` must name the panel's id — and two independently
// assembled halves are two answers to "which element does this button open".
type DialogView struct {
	// ID is the panel's element id, and the panel's heading id is derived from
	// it. It must be unique in the document. Required: a dialog whose id is empty
	// cannot be pointed at, so `aria-controls` would name nothing and the button
	// would be a focus stop that opens nothing — §7.7's "a popup that cannot open
	// is a dead control".
	ID string
	// TriggerLabel is the button's visible text.
	TriggerLabel string
	// Title is the dialog's own heading, and therefore its accessible name.
	// §7.7 says "labelled by their heading", so there is no separate name field:
	// a dialog whose name differs from its heading is a dialog with two truths.
	Title string
	// Description is optional supporting text under the title. Rendered inside
	// the scroll region, not as `aria-describedby`, because it is read as part of
	// the dialog and it is short.
	Description string
	// Body is the dialog's content. It carries whatever controls the caller needs;
	// a dialog's *actions* belong in here rather than in a list on this struct,
	// because every action is a form or a button the caller already knows how to
	// render and §7.7 says nothing about them.
	Body templ.Component
	// TestID is the panel's test hook.
	TestID string
}

// Dialog renders a trigger and the modal panel it opens, as one fragment.
//
// `aria-modal="true"` is asserted here rather than added by the script that
// traps focus, because the attribute is what tells assistive technology the rest
// of the page is unreachable. A dialog that traps focus without it is a keyboard
// trap with no announcement, which §8.1's tier-4 rules exist to prevent.
//
// `hidden` in the served markup, always. A dialog open on page load traps focus
// with no script to release it — the one failure mode a modal has that no other
// component has. Phase 9's script removes the attribute to open it.
//
// `tabindex="-1"` on the panel so focus can land on the dialog itself rather
// than on its first control: §7.2's reasoning for its skip-link targets applies
// verbatim, and a reader who hears a dialog with no name and no position loses
// both. Negative, because §7.10 prohibits a positive `tabindex` outright and a
// dialog that added itself to the tab order would be in it twice.
//
// The close button is here rather than in Body for the same reason the frame is:
// §7.7 requires `Escape` to close and focus to return to the trigger, and a
// dialog with no visible close is a dialog only a keyboard user can dismiss.
templ Dialog(view DialogView) {
	<button
		type="button"
		class={ TargetClass + " button" }
		aria-haspopup="dialog"
		aria-expanded="false"
		aria-controls={ view.ID }
		data-dialog-trigger={ view.ID }
		data-testid={ view.ID + "-trigger" }
	>
		{ view.TriggerLabel }
	</button>
	<div
		id={ view.ID }
		class="dialog"
		role="dialog"
		aria-modal="true"
		aria-labelledby={ view.ID + "-title" }
		tabindex="-1"
		hidden
		data-dialog={ view.ID }
		data-testid={ view.TestID }
	>
		<h2 class="dialog-title" id={ view.ID + "-title" }>{ view.Title }</h2>
		// §11's named failure: "the 580px centre height is unforgiving —
		// vertical overflow on a dialog", mitigated by "every dialog is
		// height-capped and internally scrollable". The cap is a CSS property and
		// lives in the stylesheet; what this package owns is the *region*: one
		// element that scrolls, so the stylesheet has something to make
		// scrollable and the title and the close button stay put while it does.
		<div class="dialog-body" data-dialog-scroll="true">
			if view.Description != "" {
				<p class="dialog-description">{ view.Description }</p>
			}
			if view.Body != nil {
				@view.Body
			}
		</div>
		<div class="dialog-actions">
			<button type="button" class={ TargetClass + " button" } data-dialog-close={ view.ID } data-testid={ view.ID + "-close" }>
				Close
			</button>
		</div>
	</div>
}